<h3 id="who-holds">Who holds these views and why</h3>

<p>Ideas rarely arrive alone. Each part of the thesis echoes arguments made, for their own reasons, by people who have never heard of RCE. We list them as convergence observations: agreement in direction, never confirmation of our claims. Where we read the source in full we quote it with its section; where we did not, we say so and do not quote.</p>

<p><strong>Compression as understanding (T1, T2).</strong> Solomonoff, Kolmogorov and Chaitin defined complexity as shortest description [T3, T4, T21]; Rissanen made it a model selection rule [T6]; Hutter's prize and AIXI and Sutskever's talks extend it to intelligence (not read here; we do not quote them). Delétang and colleagues at DeepMind argue that "a model that compresses well generalizes well" [T7, Sec. 1] and show large language models compress images and audio. Schmidhuber argues that if the history of the universe were computable, its simplest explanation "would be the shortest program that computes it" [T8, Sec. 1]. Disagreement: Delétang et al. warn that "scaling beyond a certain point will deteriorate the compression performance since the model parameters need to be accounted for" [T7, Sec. 1], and Chollet argues that "solely measuring skill at any given task falls short of measuring intelligence" [T22, abstract]; compression of a fixed corpus is not the same as efficient learning of new skills.</p>

<p><strong>Computation as physics (T3).</strong> Landauer [T9] (not read here; known through Bennett), Bennett [T10] and Lloyd [T11] hold that information processing is a physical process with energy costs and limits. Lloyd: "Energy limits speed. Entropy limits memory." [T11, Sec. 1.5]. Wolpert's stochastic thermodynamics treats computation, including finite automata, as non-equilibrium physics [T12, Secs. I to II, partial read]. Fredkin and Toffoli's conservative logic [T23], Feynman's lectures on computation, Wheeler's "it from bit" and Wolfram's computational universe point the same way (not read here). Disagreement: Bennett records objections that Landauer's principle is false, or, being a consequence of the second law of thermodynamics, either unnecessary or insufficient to exorcise Maxwell's demon, and answers them [T10]; the debate is about foundations, not about the size of the floor.</p>

<p><strong>State machines and deterministic semantics (T1, T3).</strong> Turing's machines [T19], Milner's calculus [T24], Harel's statecharts [T25], Lamport's TLA, which treats a program as a set of behaviours over states [T26], the synchronous languages Esterel and Lustre (Berry, Halbwachs) [T27, T28], Elliott's functional reactive programming [T29], the Elm architecture, deterministic simulation testing at FoundationDB and TigerBeetle, and Hoare's and Dijkstra's view of programs as predicates [T30] all treat a program as a state machine with exact semantics. We cite these as history; none were read in full for this section, so none are quoted. Limits: Turing [T19] and Rice [T20] bound what can be decided about arbitrary programs; deterministic semantics is easy to state for a closed language and hard to keep across real hardware, threads and networks.</p>

<p><strong>Programs as data on a low-dimensional manifold (T2).</strong> Hindle et al. found software "natural", more repetitive than English [T15] (not read in full here). Alon et al.'s code2vec maps code to vectors where "semantically similar objects are mapped to close vectors" [T16, Sec. 1]. Sketch-based synthesis (Solar-Lezama), DreamCoder's library learning (Ellis, Tenenbaum, cited in the history above) and Allamanis's survey of machine learning on code point the same way (Solar-Lezama and Allamanis not read here). Disagreement: code2vec's authors report that "when given uninformative, obfuscated or adversarial variable names, the prediction of the label is usually less accurate" [T16, Sec. 7]: learned embeddings can track names rather than meaning, which is the gap a canonical Form is meant to close.</p>

<p><strong>World models and latent prediction (T4).</strong> LeCun's JEPA programme at Meta FAIR (cited above), Ha and Schmidhuber, who "learn a compressed spatial and temporal representation of the environment" [T14, abstract], MuZero [T13], physics-informed neural networks, where prior physical knowledge "can act as a regularization agent that constrains the space of admissible solutions to a manageable size" [T31, Sec. 1], and Hamiltonian neural networks, motivated by models whose simulation "drifts over time to higher or lower energy states" [T32, Sec. 1], all build structure into learned dynamics. Lagrangian networks (Cranmer), Dreamer (Hafner) and Koopman operator learning share the aim (not read here). Disagreement: Ha and Schmidhuber found that a learned world model "will be exploitable by the controller, even if in the actual environment such exploits do not exist" [T14, Sec. 4.5]; Greydanus et al. note their method assumes a conserved quantity and so cannot model friction [T32, Sec. 3.2]. For code, the checker is the guard against exploiting the model's errors.</p>

<p><strong>Energy-based models (T4).</strong> Hopfield [T33] and Hinton [T34] framed learning as shaping an energy surface (not read here); Du and Mordatch [T17] show implicit generation by descending a learned energy, and LeCun (cited above) places energy minimization at the centre of JEPA inference. Disagreement within the field concerns training stability and sampling cost, which Du and Mordatch discuss [T17].</p>

